Tidal Flood Risk & Infrastructure Vulnerability Engine
Project Overview & Study Area
Primary Study Area: Lagos–Calabar Coastal Highway Corridor (Phase 1 focus: Lagos State Atlantic Shoreline through the Niger 
delta coast
Area of Interest (AOI): A locked $5\text{ km}$ spatial buffer along the official highway alignment vector (EPSG:32631/ UTM 
Part 1 — Research Question
How can an automated geospatial screening engine delineate low-elevation coastal flood risk zones (< 5 metres elevation),
quantify potential human population displacement, and evaluate economic asset exposure along the l Highway corridor over time?
Why It Matters
The 700 km highway intersects fragile estuarine ecosystems, barrier islands, and dense coastal settlements exposed to storm surges
sea-level rise, and coastal erosion. Civil engineers, disaster management agencies,, and climate adaptation planners require an 
automated system to:
       Simulate multi-depth flood scenarios (1m to 5m)
       Pinpoint high-vulnerability road segments.
       Target coastal protection investments without relying on manual, non-reproducible desktop GIS tasks.
